From: yasoteien <263659156+yasoteien@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mfd: rk8xx: Register a final power-off handler so that poweroff does not halt

The RK8xx driver writes DEV_OFF in the SYS_OFF_MODE_POWER_OFF_PREPARE
step (over I2C, which may sleep) and registers no handler for
SYS_OFF_MODE_POWER_OFF.
On a board where nothing else can power off (no PSCI SYSTEM_OFF, no
gpio-poweroff), kernel_can_power_off() is then false, and
reboot(LINUX_REBOOT_CMD_POWER_OFF) is turned into LINUX_REBOOT_CMD_HALT
before kernel_power_off() is ever called:

  - "systemctl poweroff" halts the CPUs and leaves the PMIC on. The board
    stays on, draining the battery, until a watchdog resets it or the
    power button is held.
  - When kernel_power_off() is called directly (hw_protection's forced
    shutdown, orderly_poweroff(true)), DEV_OFF is written in the prepare
    step, but machine_power_off() then finds no handler and returns at
    once. hw_failure_emergency_action_func() goes on to
    emergency_restart(), which can win against the PMIC's power-down
    sequence, so the board restarts instead of switching off.

Seen on a Pomera DM250 (RK3128 + RK818): every "systemctl poweroff" on
battery ended in a halt and a watchdog reset 57 s later; markers in the
GRF showed kernel_shutdown_prepare(SYSTEM_HALT), and DEV_OFF was never
written.

Register a handler for the final step as well, at SYS_OFF_PRIO_LOW so
that any real power-off handler runs first. It only waits up to 5 s for
the DEV_OFF written in the prepare step to take effect, and then returns,
leaving the fallback to the caller as before.

Signed-off-by: yasoteien <263659156+yasoteien@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/drivers/mfd/rk8xx-core.c
+++ b/drivers/mfd/rk8xx-core.c
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <linux/bitfield.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
 #include <linux/mfd/rk808.h>
 #include <linux/mfd/core.h>
@@ -654,6 +655,23 @@
 	return NOTIFY_DONE;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The supplies are switched off in the prepare step, where the I2C bus may
+ * still sleep. This handler only waits for that to take effect, so that
+ * machine_power_off() does not return while the PMIC is still on.
+ */
+#define RK8XX_POWER_OFF_WAIT_MS		5000
+
+static int rk808_power_off_wait(struct sys_off_data *data)
+{
+	struct rk808 *rk808 = data->cb_data;
+
+	mdelay(RK8XX_POWER_OFF_WAIT_MS);
+	dev_emerg(rk808->dev, "still running %d ms after the power-off request\n",
+		  RK8XX_POWER_OFF_WAIT_MS);
+	return NOTIFY_DONE;
+}
+
 static int rk808_restart(struct sys_off_data *data)
 {
 	struct rk808 *rk808 = data->cb_data;
@@ -818,6 +836,19 @@
 			return dev_err_probe(dev, ret,
 					     "failed to register poweroff handler\n");
 
+		/*
+		 * Without a handler for the final power-off step,
+		 * kernel_can_power_off() is false when nothing else (PSCI,
+		 * gpio-poweroff, ...) can power off, and reboot(POWER_OFF)
+		 * is turned into a halt with the PMIC still on.
+		 */
+		ret = devm_register_sys_off_handler(dev,
+				    SYS_OFF_MODE_POWER_OFF, SYS_OFF_PRIO_LOW,
+				    &rk808_power_off_wait, rk808);
+		if (ret)
+			return dev_err_probe(dev, ret,
+					     "failed to register poweroff wait handler\n");
+
 		switch (rk808->variant) {
 		case RK809_ID:
 		case RK817_ID:
